<wdkModel>

  <paramSet name="StrainSegmentParams">

    <!-- multiPick, so ONE search returns one segment per selected strain rather than
         needing N searches for N strains.  The ID query
         (StrainSegmentId.StrainSegmentsByRefSegment) unnests the selection and mints one
         primary key per strain; everything downstream was already multi record capable
         (StrainSegmentAttributes.Coords keys on source_id, BedReporter streams one line
         per record), so multiPick is the whole of the change on the input side.

         quote="true" matters and must stay.  With it, EnumParamHandler.toInternalValue
         quotes each internal value and joins them with DBPlatform.prepareExpressionList,
         which on PostgreSQL (FgpUtil PostgreSQL.java) is a plain comma join.  So
         $$strain$$ renders as   'A17-10A-1','A17-3C-11',...   which is exactly an
         ARRAY[...] element list.  Drop the quote and it renders bare and unparseable.

         maxSelectedCount="500" is a correctness rail, not a performance one:
           a) It is above the ENTIRE vocabulary as it stands (452 strains on unidb_shu_a,
              the live appDb, measured 2026-07-31), so it refuses nothing a user can ask
              for today; selecting every strain still works.
           b) It sits below Oracle's EXPRESSION_LIMIT of 999 (FgpUtil Oracle.java).  Above
              that limit prepareExpressionList stops emitting a comma list and starts
              emitting "SELECT * FROM table(SYS.DBMS_DEBUG_VC2COLL(...)) UNION ...", which
              ARRAY[...] cannot consume, so the ID query would break.  UniDB is PostgreSQL
              and so is not exposed today, but the cap makes that cliff unreachable by
              construction instead of by luck of how big the vocabulary happens to be.
           c) It bounds the ID query's reject path, whose cost is linear in
              (selected strains x that strain's indel row count).  Measured on unidb_shu_a
              2026-07-31: 1 strain accepts in ~0.35 ms, 5 in ~0.61 ms, and all 452 in
              ~1.9 s, with the all reject case ~1.9 s as well.

         allowEmpty is left at its default of false (Param.java), so WDK rejects an empty
         selection during validation and ARRAY[] (which PostgreSQL cannot type) is never
         generated. -->
    <flatVocabParam name="strain"
                    queryRef="StrainSegmentVQ.StrainsWithIndelData"
                    prompt="Strain / isolate"
                    multiPick="true"
                    maxSelectedCount="500">
      <help>
        One or more strains or isolates whose coordinate systems the returned segments
        are expressed in: the search returns one segment per selected strain. The list is
        all strains that have indel data loaded. A selected strain that does not belong to
        the reference sequence's organism is dropped from the result rather than being an
        error.
      </help>
    </flatVocabParam>

  </paramSet>

  <!--===========================================================================-->
  <!--  Vocab queries                                                            -->
  <!--===========================================================================-->

  <querySet name="StrainSegmentVQ" queryType="vocab" isCacheable="true">

    <!-- Strain identity is protocolappnode.name minus the '_Indel' suffix; there is
         no strain column on apidb.indel.  The vocabulary is global: the search
         takes no organism param, and relevance is enforced instead by the ID
         query's EXISTS gate (gate 3 there), which scopes a chosen strain to the
         reference sequence's ORGANISM.  It deliberately does NOT require indel data
         on that exact sequence: zero indels on a contig is the ordinary
         identity-mapping case, not a missing-data case (see spec 5.1.1).

         Driven from study.protocolappnode with an EXISTS probe into apidb.indel,
         rather than the reverse: an earlier revision derived the candidate set from
         a DISTINCT scan of every apidb.indel row, which
         dominated its cost. Probing indel from the protocolappnode side lets the
         planner use indel's protocol_app_node_id index instead of scanning it.
         That also means regexp_replace now runs over only the rows matching the
         name pattern rather than the full indel row count, so the earlier
         requirement to dedupe on protocol_app_node_id before applying
         regexp_replace no longer applies here.

         Row-count provenance, because two databases get confused here.  The 61,512
         protocolappnode rows and 43.6M apidb.indel rows quoted by the original of
         this comment were measured on genomicsdb_rebuild01, a DIFFERENT and larger
         database.  On unidb_shu_a (the live appDb, measured 2026-07-31) it is 1,356
         protocolappnode rows and 1,855,449 indel rows, of which 452 nodes carry the
         '_Indel' suffix.  The shape argument above holds at either scale; only the
         magnitude of the win changes. -->
    <sqlQuery name="StrainsWithIndelData">
      <column name="internal"/>
      <column name="term"/>
      <column name="display"/>
      <sql>
        <![CDATA[
          SELECT strain AS internal, strain AS term, strain AS display
          FROM (
            SELECT DISTINCT regexp_replace(pan.name, '_Indel$', '') AS strain
            FROM study.protocolappnode pan
            WHERE pan.name LIKE '%\_Indel'
              AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM apidb.indel i
                          WHERE i.protocol_app_node_id = pan.protocol_app_node_id)
          ) t
          ORDER BY strain
        ]]>
      </sql>
    </sqlQuery>

  </querySet>

</wdkModel>
